[164.91238, "o", "  ⚙ bash python3 -c 'import pip; print(pip.__file__, pip.__version__)' 2>&1; ec…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 24 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: ls -d /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-* 2>&1; echo \"---EXIT:$?\" — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash ls -d /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip /usr/local/lib/pytho…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 25 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: ls -ld /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash ls -ld /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info 2>…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 26 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ All four verification checks pass. Now the dry-run test (which needs netw…\r\n[approval] bash: python3 -m pip install --dry-run --no-input --quiet requests 2>&1; echo \"---EXIT:$?\" — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash python3 -m pip install --dry-run --no-input --quiet requests 2>&1; ech…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r"]
[164.912407, "o", "\n  qwen3.6-35b — 27 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ The dry-run resolved `requests` successfully (exit 0, no errors — only a …\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 28 tool call(s) so far\r\n  ⚙ todo \r\n    ✓ todo — 4/4 done\r\n  [tools] 28 call(s) without changing a file — nudging to decide\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 28 tool call(s) so far\r\n  ⚙ task \r\n    ⚠ task implementor — 28 tool call(s)\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 15 tool call(s) so far\r\nFixed. Here's what was wrong and what I did.\r\n\r\n**Cause.** Your `pip` failure was not a permissions, PEP 668, network, or config problem — it was a corrupt install. `/usr/local/bin/python3` is Python 3.13.1 (prefix `/usr/local`), and `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages` still had `pip-24.3.1.dist-info/` while the actual `pip/` package directory was gone. The `pip`/`pip3` scripts on disk were fine, so every invocation died on `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'`. (`python3 -m pip` died the same way, which is what steers people toward re-downloading get-pip.py or using sudo."]
[164.912412, "o", ") No pip config files, no proxy/SSL env overrides, no pyenv/conda shim, and 171 GB free — all ruled out by inspection.\r\n\r\n**Repair.** Reinstalled pip from Python's own bundled `ensurepip` wheel (`pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl`) — no network, no apt, no `get-pip.py`. `ensurepip --upgrade` alone refused to act at first: it saw the orphaned `.dist-info` and reported \"Requirement already satisfied\". Removing that stale directory and re-running let ensurepip extract `pip/` properly. Same version, 24.3.1; the old dist-info was replaced and the package code is now actually on disk. The system Python 3.11 at `/usr/bin/python3.11` was untouched.\r\n\r\n**Verified.**\r\n- `python3 -m pip --version` → `pip 24.3.1 from /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip (python 3.13)`\r\n- `pip --version` and `pip3 --version` → same, so the scripts that were crashing now work\r\n- `python3 -c 'import pip'` → exit 0\r\n- `python3 -m pip install --dry-run requests` → resolves cleanly (nothing installed)\r\n\r\n**Two things to keep in mi"]
[164.912416, "o", "nd.** Debian's `/usr/lib/python3.11/EXTERNALLY-MANAGED` marker is still in place, so `python3.11 -m pip install` will still be refused by design — use `apt` or a venv for that interpreter. And since `/usr/local/bin/python3` is a hand-built install rather than an externally-managed one, package installs there go straight into `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages`; for project work a venv is still the cleaner habit. Nothing in this repository was read for the diagnosis beyond the harness surface, and nothing in it was modified — it holds only `.gnomon/` and no Python code or environment.\r\n\r\n[refusal] role=coordinator model=deepseek-v4.1-flash tools=15 surface=34a4546ddc81\r\n"]
